    "67": "Page 67. IMAGINED Written for this case, based on real events, not a real document. West of Sydney, looking at the mountains May 1813 · a reconstruction · not a historical document From the colony, the Blue Mountains look like a wall. Maps of the settled country end there, as if the land itself stopped. It did not stop. People had been crossing those ridges for thousands of years. The question was never wh",
    "68": "Page 68. A NOTE BEFORE YOU READ This case is set on Darug, Gundungurra and Wiradjuri Country. It discusses colonisation, dispossession and the Bathurst War, and it names people who have died. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers may wish to take care. No photographs of deceased people are used. A note for teachers is at the end of the case. CROSSING THE BLUE MOUNTAINS, 1813 · YEAR 9 · READ Crossin",
    "69": "Page 69. Lawson, a trained surveyor, recorded compass bearings, times and distances so that others could retrace the track. On 29 May they descended into what Lawson called “a fine grazing Country.” They rested on 30 May. On 31 May they went further west, then turned for home. Lawson’s reason is plain: “our shoes worn out and provisions nearly Expended Obliged us to Return home.” They had found and marked ",
    "70": "Page 70. thousands of years. European numbers around Bathurst were still small at first (about 114 in 1820) but had grown to more than 1,200 by 1824. After killings on both sides, Governor Brisbane declared martial law around Bathurst on 14 August 1824. Soldiers and settlers attacked Wiradjuri people in what is now called the Bathurst War. Windradyne, a Wiradjuri leader, resisted and later sought peace. Ev",
    "71": "Page 71. Three witnesses CROSSING THE BLUE MOUNTAINS, 1813 · YEAR 9 · ASK Each witness is a real person, or a real set of words from the record. A period portrait is used where one exists and is free to reuse. Otherwise you will see a plain silhouette, not a likeness. Ask the same three questions of each. Gregory Blaxland Organiser of the 1813 expedition · A Journal of a Tour of Discovery Across the Blue M",
    "72": "Page 72. William Lawson Trained surveyor on the 1813 expedition · Journal of an Expedition Across the Blue Mountains, 11 May–6 June 1813 He logged bearings, times and distances each day. Because he wrote down how they travelled, Evans could retrace the track months later and Cox could build a road on it. What they said On 11 May: “Mr. Blaxland Wentworth and myself with four men and four A document silhouet",
    "73": "Page 73. The old routes Published accounts of Darug and Gundungurra knowledge of the mountains · and archaeology at Dargan Shelter The third witness never wrote a journal for 1813, because the knowledge was carried in story, song and practice. This file uses published accounts only. What they said The National Museum of Australia records that Gundungurra, Dharawal, A path silhouette, not a map Wiradjuri, W",
    "74": "Page 74. My finding CROSSING THE BLUE MOUNTAINS, 1813 · YEAR 9 · DECIDE There is no answer at the back. The opening scene is a reconstruction. The dates, the quotations and the road are from the records. Write one sentence on what the records show about the 1813 crossing, and one sentence on something they do not settle. You can also test three readings historians argue for: A — that Blaxland, Lawson and W",
    "75": "Page 75. Why it still matters The road over the mountains became a template: find a route, survey it, build a road, grant the land, move the stock in. Bathurst, then the gold rushes of 1851, then towns across the plains all followed the line cut in 1813–15. Words do work. “Discovery,” “opening up” and “new frontier” all assume the land was closed, empty and waiting. The record shows a peopled, managed land",
    "76": "Page 76. CROSSING THE BLUE MOUNTAINS, 1813 · YEAR 9 · SUPER SLEUTHS Super Sleuths Go further only if you want to. Amber marks what is still disputed, or what later writers added. Work on paper. Each investigation names the records, or the kind of record, to look for. STILL IN DISPUTE, OR ADDED LATER 1. The ridge method Earlier expeditions followed the rivers and failed; the 1813 party followed the ridges a",
    "77": "Page 77. STILL IN DISPUTE, OR ADDED LATER 4. Sure, maybe, or a guess? Sort each claim. “Blaxland, Lawson and Wentworth were the first people ever to cross the Blue Mountains.” “Lawson’s bearings made the route repeatable.” “Evans reached the site of Bathurst in December 1813.” “The 1813 party followed one named Aboriginal track, and we can point to it on a modern map.” For each one, write Sure, Maybe or Gu",
    "78": "Page 78. Teacher Note CROSSING THE BLUE MOUNTAINS, 1813 · YEAR 9 · TEACHER NOTE This note is for the teacher. It is not a student page. What’s true about the opener This note is not part of the student page. The opening scene is a reconstruction and carries an Imagined badge. No particular viewer, map or day is meant. What is true behind it: from the Cumberland Plain the mountains did look like a wall to t",
    "79": "Page 79. Pictures The three witness pictures are plain document or path silhouettes with a name card, not likenesses of anyone. No photograph of a deceased person is used, and no rock art is shown. A writing-draft illustration of the 1813 party on the ridge invented likenesses of real people and is not used here. Source conflicts, flagged The National Museum of Australia’s Defining Moments page, which is o",
    "80": "Page 80. (where it disagrees with the journals, the journals are preferred). “The earliest evidence of high- elevation ice age occupation in Australia,” Nature Human Behaviour, 16 June 2025. Australian Dictionary of Biography, Gregory Blaxland; State Library of New South Wales notes on Lawson. Resources There is no separate app for this case. Students can read Lawson’s journal transcript and Blaxland’s 182",
